# Skilled agents and `./src`

Supersedes and tightens the prior “skills write-and-exec” contract for Cursor skills under `.cursor/skills/`.

## Required behavior

1. **Reuse `./src`** — Every skill must execute project code under `./src` (`python -m src…`, `python src/run/…`, documented wrappers). Do not reimplement windowing, ID maps, split, train, or metrics in-chat.
2. **Edit then same logic** — If a skill must edit `./src`, keep the **same general function** and observable contracts so legacy callers and prior runs do not break. Prefer additive flags with legacy defaults.
3. **No parallel “new” functions** — Do **not** invent a generally new function when an existing module already covers the same role (e.g. do not add a second ID mapper beside `id_rule`). Extend or call the existing API.
4. **Protect `./src/run`** — Any change to shared library code must not break scripts under `./src/run/` (re-runnable agent outputs). Update those runners in the same task when needed.
5. **Novel edits → pytest** — Changing previously written `./src` functions requires running relevant pytest before COMPLETED.
6. **Record decisions** — Intentional behavior changes go in `method-decision.md`.

## Examples

```
❌ BAD
Skill re-derives gene windows in prose and writes a one-off script outside src/.

✅ GOOD
Skill writes src/run/preprocess_<data>.py that calls src.pipeline.adapt / parse_data / parse_target, then execs it.
```

```
❌ BAD
Add src/pipeline/id_map2.py because id_rule “is awkward.”

✅ GOOD
Call src.pipeline.id_rule.run_id_rule; only extend id_rule if the contract is insufficient.
```
